= May 2024 — The judge who held the clearance

**Tomasz Szmydt**, a judge of a Polish administrative court, appears at a
Minsk press conference and requests protection from Belarus. He had held
access to classified material and had sat on cases touching the security
services. He was also a familiar name inside the judicial conflict of the
previous years, which means every camp can find a version of the story that
indicts the other.

Three separate failures are visible at once: a clearance system that did not
notice, a court that seated him, and a public argument about judicial
legitimacy so exhausted that a defection can be absorbed into it as just
another talking point.

[? if surveillance_scandal_stage >= 2: We already forced the surveillance file
open once, in 2021, which means we know how long it takes for a technical
finding to reach a courtroom and how badly the wait is reported.?]
[? if infosec_standard = 1: The information-security standard we wrote after
the 2021 mailbox leak covers ministries and chancelleries. It does not cover a
single judge, which is the gap this month found.?]
[? if left_in_government = 1: The clearance service reports to this
government. Whatever we say about the previous one, the vetting failure is now
ours to repair.?][? if left_in_government = 0: We hold no security portfolio.
We can demand the vetting review, defend the courts from collective suspicion,
or use the case against the camp that appointed him.?]

