@szmydt_vetting
title: Force a review of clearance and classified access across the judiciary
subtitle: Treats it as a counter-intelligence failure; the review will find more than anyone wants published.
on-arrival: {!
Q.szmydt_response = "Clearance review";
Q.security_incident_pressure = Math.max(
    0, (Number(Q.security_incident_pressure) || 0) - 4
);
Q.institutional_trust = (Number(Q.institutional_trust) || 0) + 5;
Q.administrative_reform_pressure =
    (Number(Q.administrative_reform_pressure) || 0) + 4;
Q.public_trust = (Number(Q.public_trust) || 0) + 2;
Q.civil_military_trust = (Number(Q.civil_military_trust) || 50) + 3;
Q.news_headline =
    "A clearance review follows the judge rather than the argument about him";
!}

We ask the narrow question: who holds classified access inside the judicial
system, who granted it, when was it last reviewed, and what happens when a
holder travels. It is the question the services would ask themselves, which is
why it produces an answer instead of a headline.

Reviews of this kind always find more than the case that triggered them. Some
of what it finds will embarrass judges we have defended for years, and we have
just committed ourselves to publishing the standard rather than the exception.

- @poland_event_queue: Order the clearance question

@szmydt_courts
title: Defend the courts from collective suspicion while isolating the case
subtitle: Protects judicial legitimacy at the cost of looking protective of a system that produced him.
on-arrival: judicial_legitimacy += 6; judicial_repair_mandate += 3; institutional_trust += 3; public_trust += 1; russian_influence_salience -= 4; konf_poll_momentum += 0.1; news_headline = "The Left refuses to let one defection stand for the judiciary"

One judge asked a dictatorship for shelter. Ten thousand did not, and several
of them have spent eight years being disciplined for rulings the government
disliked. We say so plainly, and we say it in the week when saying it is
unpopular.

The anti-system channels have exactly the material they want: the Left
defending judges again. The judicial institutions we still need for every
repair we have promised are worth the cost of that clip.

- @poland_event_queue: Publish the defence of the courts

@szmydt_blame
title: Make the appointment itself the charge against the camp that made it
subtitle: The available partisan attack; it accepts that judicial legitimacy is now a campaign asset rather than an institution.
on-arrival: pis_relation -= 8; russian_influence_salience += 6; left_poll_momentum += 0.15; judicial_legitimacy -= 4; institutional_trust -= 3; public_trust += 1; media_capacity += 2; news_headline = "The defection becomes an argument about who appointed him"

The line is easy: the people who spent eight years rebuilding the courts
around loyalty produced a judge who defected to Minsk. It travels, it is
partly true, and it makes the following week considerably more comfortable.

It also teaches the country that judicial appointments are a party scandal
rather than a constitutional problem, which is the premise every future
majority will use to justify its own appointments.

- @poland_event_queue: Take the appointment argument to the chamber
